1|It can cause illness.\n2|Clean water\n3|Drinking water with no change at all\n4|A pure stream that never touches the ground\n5|What pollution?\n6|Soil.\n7|Materials to soil.\n8|Soil in not materials.\n9|From not plants.\n10|Pollution materials to soil (, ) to plants living.\n11|In soil in the.\n12|On soil.\n13|A large city\n14|A set of living things, their habitat, and the links among them\n15|A forest of plants with no animals or water\n16|Wildlife with no plants or food cycles\n17|An ecosystem includes living things, their surroundings, and how they interact.\n18|A change in the environment can affect every part of the system.\n19|What is a food web?\n20|A list of all the foods found in one habitat.\n21|A diagram showing how energy flows through many connected food chains in an ecosystem.\n22|A web that spiders build to catch food near plants.\n23|A model that shows only cold-blooded animals and their energy needs.\n24|A food web shows how energy moves through an ecosystem by connecting many food chains. It shows how plants, herbivores, and carnivores are all linked.\n25|Plants are the producers at the base of most food webs.\n26|Each part of the food web depends on the other parts.\n27|What is one good way to help protect the environment?\n28|Use as many natural resources as possible each day.\n29|Recycle, save water, and reduce the amount of waste you produce.\n30|Throw all trash away as quickly as possible in large amounts.\n31|Cut down trees to create more open space for buildings.\n32|We can protect the environment by making smart daily choices: recycling materials, conserving water, and reducing waste all help reduce our impact on nature.\n33|Small everyday actions like recycling and saving water add up to a big difference.\n34|Everyone can help the environment by being careful about how they use resources.\n35|Which action best helps reduce waste and protect the environment?\n36|Throw all garbage into one trash bag without sorting it\n37|Reuse and recycle materials instead of throwing them away\n38|Dump old materials into rivers or lakes\n39|Buy new products every time instead of repairing old ones\n40|Reusing and recycling materials reduces waste and helps protect the environment by keeping harmful items out of landfills and waterways.\n41|Reducing waste keeps the environment clean and healthy.\n42|Recycling turns used materials into new products, saving energy and resources.\n43|In a fair test, what do we call a factor that can be changed or measured?\n44|The final result that is recorded at the very end of the experiment.\n45|A factor in an experiment that can be changed or measured to see how it affects the result.\n46|Everything that is NOT included in the experiment at all.\n47|The equipment used to carry out the experiment.\n48|A variable is something that can change in an experiment. Scientists change or measure variables to learn what affects the results.\n49|In a fair test, usually only one variable is changed at a time.\n50|Other conditions are kept the same so the comparison is fair.\n51|What is a dependent variable?\n52|The variable the researcher plans to change on purpose\n53|What you measure as the result of the change\n54|Only the sample size, with no link to recorded values\n55|Data you ignore to save measuring time\n56|The dependent variable is what you measure to see the result of changing the independent variable.\n57|It is what you want to find out.\n58|It is the outcome of the experiment.\n59|What is a control group?\n60|A group where you change every factor at once with no baseline for comparison\n61|A group you compare other results against\n62|A group measured only at the end with no starting treatment\n63|A group meant to copy the main result with no baseline\n64|A control group is left unchanged so you can compare results and see what made the difference.\n65|It is an important part of a science experiment.\n66|What is a conclusion?\n67|Ending the experiment with no explanation of results\n68|What you learned from the experiment and what it means\n69|An opening with no data analysis\n70|A middle step with no link to the question\n71|A conclusion explains what you learned from the experiment and what the results mean.\n72|Conclusions help you understand what was learned.\n73|They are an important part of a science experiment.\n74|What does safety in experiments mean?\n75|Partial caution with no advance preparation\n76|Being careful and making sure everything is safe before, during, and after\n77|Doing steps quickly with no equipment check\n78|Only having fun with no protective rules\n79|Experiment safety means being careful and checking that everything is safe before, during, and after the experiment.\n80|Safety is the most important rule.\n81|An adult should always help.\n82|Why is daytime brighter than nighttime?\n83|The Sun is up and provides light and warmth during the day.\n84|The Moon always reflects more sunlight than the Sun during the day.\n85|Stars shine just as brightly during the night as the Sun does during the day.\n86|Daytime and nighttime are always equally bright.\n87|During the day, the side of Earth you are on faces the Sun. The Sun is a powerful source of light and heat. At night, your side of Earth faces away from the Sun, so it is darker and cooler.\n88|The Sun is Earth's main source of light and energy.\n89|Earth's rotation causes the switch between day and night every 24 hours.\n90|Which statement about Earth and the Sun is true?\n91|Earth travels around the Sun once every year.\n92|Earth only moves through space during the daytime.\n93|The Sun and Moon revolve around each other.\n94|Earth and the Sun are both completely still in space.\n95|Earth moves around the Sun in a path called an orbit. One full orbit takes about 365 days — one year. Earth also spins on its own axis, which is what creates day and night over a 24-hour cycle.\n96|Earth's yearly journey around the Sun is called a revolution.\n97|Earth's daily spin on its own axis is called rotation.\n98|What is the main difference between a star and a planet like Earth?\n99|A planet is a body that orbits a star\n100|There is no difference between them\n101|A planet is always larger than the Sun\n102|Stars are found only on the Moon\n103|A star like the Sun is a hot glowing ball that gives off its own light. A planet is cooler and orbits a star.\n104|In the solar system, Earth is a planet.\n105|The Sun is the star at the center of the solar system.\n106|What do the heart and lungs do for your body?\n107|The heart pumps blood through the body, and the lungs fill that blood with oxygen.\n108|The lungs pump blood, and the heart pulls air into the chest.\n109|Both organs break down food and turn it into energy.\n110|Both organs send signals to control how the brain works.\n111|The heart and lungs work as a team. The lungs take in oxygen from the air and pass it into the blood. The heart then pumps that oxygen-rich blood to every part of the body. Together, they keep every cell supplied with the oxygen it needs.\n112|Every time you breathe in, your lungs fill with air and pass oxygen into your bloodstream.\n113|The heart beats around 60 to 100 times per minute to keep blood flowing constantly.\n114|What causes daytime on Earth?\n115|Earth rotating so that our side faces toward the Sun.\n116|The Sun moving to the far side of the Moon each night.\n117|The Moon blocking sunlight and bouncing it back toward Earth.\n118|Earth staying still while the Sun circles around it.\n119|Earth spins on its axis, making one full turn every 24 hours. When the side of Earth where you live faces the Sun, it is daytime. When that side faces away from the Sun, it is nighttime.\n120|Earth's rotation is what creates the 24-hour cycle of day and night.\n121|The Sun itself does not move around Earth — Earth's spin makes it look like the Sun rises and sets.\n122|Which action helps protect a garden or park?\n123|Pick up trash and put it in a bin instead of leaving it on the grass\n124|Break branches off every tree you pass\n125|Leave a water faucet running all the time\n126|Paint on rocks without asking an adult\n127|Picking up trash and using a bin cuts pollution and helps protect plants and animals.\n128|Everyone can help keep outdoor places clean and safe.\n129|Trash can pollute soil and water.\n130|What is a simple difference between a hard material and a soft material when you try to bend it?\n131|A hard material is harder to bend without breaking\n132|There is never any difference between them\n133|Only the size of the piece matters\n134|A hard material is always a liquid\n135|Hardness and softness are properties you can feel: soft materials bend more easily, while hard ones resist bending or may snap.\n136|We learn about materials by looking at them and touching them carefully.\n137|Materials can be hard, soft, flexible, or brittle.\n138|What is the main difference between a plant and a rock?\n139|A plant grows and needs water and light\n140|Both grow in the same way\n141|A rock grows like a tree if you pour water on it\n142|There is no difference between living and nonliving things\n143|A plant is a living thing that grows and responds to conditions. A rock is nonliving and does not grow like a plant.\n144|Living things grow and respond to their surroundings; nonliving things do not.\n145|Plants need water, light, and air to grow.\n146|What is the main job of the heart in your body?\n147|It pumps blood to all parts of the body\n148|It helps you breathe air in and out\n149|The heart never changes how fast it beats\n150|It controls how quickly you breathe during exercise\n151|The heart is a muscle that pumps blood through the circulatory system, delivering oxygen and nutrients to every part of your body.\n152|The heart is a strong muscle that works every moment of your life.\n153|It pumps blood to the lungs to pick up oxygen, then sends it to the rest of the body.\n154|Which statement about the Sun and Earth in our solar system is true?\n155|The Sun is the largest object in our solar system and sits at its center.\n156|The Moon is the main source of daylight.\n157|Earth is at the center of the solar system.\n158|All objects in the solar system orbit the Moon.\n159|The Sun is at the center of our solar system. It is by far the largest object in the system — over a million Earths could fit inside it. All the planets, including Earth, orbit the Sun due to its strong gravitational pull.\n160|The Sun's gravity keeps all the planets moving in their orbits.\n161|Earth is the third planet from the Sun and orbits it once every 365 days.\n162|Why is plastic pollution in the ocean a serious problem?\n163|Sea animals can mistake plastic for food, which harms or kills them.\n164|Plastic makes ocean water taste better for fish.\n165|The ocean naturally breaks plastic down into clean, safe water.\n166|Plastic always sinks harmlessly to the ocean floor.\n167|Plastic that reaches the ocean breaks into smaller and smaller pieces but never fully disappears. Sea turtles, fish, and seabirds often mistake plastic for food. When they eat it, it blocks their digestion and can kill them. Plastic pollution also damages coral reefs and other marine habitats.\n168|Plastic can take hundreds of years to break down in the environment.\n169|Millions of tons of plastic enter the ocean every year, threatening marine life worldwide.\n170|What is a good order of steps for a simple science experiment?\n171|Ask a question, make a guess, test it, and record the results\n172|Finish right away without writing anything down\n173|Change every condition in the middle with no plan\n174|Only guess and never check in real life\n175|Science usually starts with a question and a guess, then a careful test and notes about what happened.\n176|A good experiment includes a short plan and a record of what you saw.\n177|Conclusions should be based on evidence from the test.\n178|Which is an example of a physical change only, with no new material made?\n179|Folding a sheet of paper without burning it\n180|Burning paper into ash\n181|Baking dough until it becomes bread\n182|Iron rusting over time\n183|Folding paper changes its shape, but it is still paper. Burning, rusting, and baking often make new materials.\n184|A physical change mostly keeps the same material and may change shape or state.\n185|A chemical change makes new materials.\n186|In simple words, what is a main job of blood in the body?\n187|Carry oxygen and nutrients and help remove waste from cells\n188|Make all energy straight from air with no help from other organs\n189|Fully replace the job of the brain and nerves\n190|Stop breathing whenever the body needs food\n191|Blood carries oxygen, nutrients, and other useful materials, and it helps move waste away from cells.\n192|The circulatory system works with breathing and digestion.\n193|The heart pumps blood through blood vessels to the whole body.\n194|Why do people on Earth experience day and night about every 24 hours?\n195|Earth spinning on its axis so that each side faces the Sun at different times.\n196|The Sun moving around Earth once every 24 hours.\n197|The Moon blocking the Sun's light to create darkness.\n198|Earth moving closer to or farther from the Sun throughout the day.\n199|Earth's rotation turns different places toward and away from the Sun, creating day and night.\n200|One full spin of Earth takes about one day.